#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59f9abf1-17db-5717-8660-a531ef00b15c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function for a Positive Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "62da71f1-5a3a-509a-a68e-0596f88c4ae4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8951" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\Gamma(7).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\Gamma(7) = 6! = 720.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8de43f-dc61-5bbe-8b84-b8aa0be19146" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at a Multiple of One-Half"}] :question/id #uuid "5d084f86-9874-5bdd-a416-dba742d7e4e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8952" :question/problem "Calculate $\\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z), \\qquad \\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) & =\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171872 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"}] :question/id #uuid "aab1e422-0ac4-59b7-bed9-5ce75b4fe22d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9033" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $\\Gamma(z)$ for $\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0?$\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{t^{z}}{te^t} \\, \\textrm dt$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{e^t}{t^{1-z}} \\, \\textrm dt$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt, \\qquad \\textrm{Re}(z) > 0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The upper bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral II matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{t^{z}}{te^t} \\, \\textrm dt = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1}e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt = \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\dfrac{e^t}{t^{1-z}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8de43f-dc61-5bbe-8b84-b8aa0be19146" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at a Multiple of One-Half"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f6601b-563c-5b31-a64c-7ec8c55ad32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432618} {:answer/id #uuid "680dd59f-269a-5239-be6c-db7639bc6aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432625} {:answer/id #uuid "6c60091c-6a50-58f6-8506-d0161e3cbf8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432626} {:answer/id #uuid "afc4fe46-ebb9-53be-a4db-c19fc81cfcc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432644} {:answer/id #uuid "e8402d97-f15b-5b34-be19-f1b09c6f80c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680dd59f-269a-5239-be6c-db7639bc6aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432625} :answer-field/id #uuid "15d1edb6-5522-5f3e-9a34-d393c62a2da0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "985175a0-3817-5f02-8236-9b18519858f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105737" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{5}{2}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59f9abf1-17db-5717-8660-a531ef00b15c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function for a Positive Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192695b8-ba15-56d7-992c-59c67d9779c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432606} {:answer/id #uuid "499ba395-f3f1-51bf-ae69-2598a54d8a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432616} {:answer/id #uuid "639720b8-fabf-54a8-9b8c-e26d7c484dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432624} {:answer/id #uuid "9313496b-f856-5477-baf7-e1d8ecc225ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432638} {:answer/id #uuid "f0511e8b-98d8-522b-b4d3-9ababe528a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192695b8-ba15-56d7-992c-59c67d9779c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432606} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9dadc3-3c7d-51dc-ae7b-ace17508786a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88939e2e-ece9-5768-a030-f015b621079a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107763" :question/problem "$Γ(12)=$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(12)=11!\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "509a5a08-2a17-53d6-bfc0-859e810cfe28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432617} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e73fb0-5e26-5b77-80d8-96d4ac256043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432653} {:answer/id #uuid "c892a373-7ca8-579b-9d31-e6a6a98294b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432654} {:answer/id #uuid "d21e8d2c-6a2c-503f-b857-9d2fb05ebfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432656} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3851a5-2ba9-5c44-a866-bd435cfdc216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc3851a5-2ba9-5c44-a866-bd435cfdc216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432662} :answer-field/id #uuid "259c3d61-272b-5770-a437-91dfc25962d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43b66f9d-fca5-54ae-b724-0bc204fa830b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107829" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9f6e2a-09b6-5d76-a955-98ec83b942b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432608} {:answer/id #uuid "3517e5b4-f44f-5fdf-86dc-8c2764fac2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432611} {:answer/id #uuid "a20ca137-1425-5eff-b510-77f79db5e4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432642} {:answer/id #uuid "b47c0721-18a4-54a8-9623-8b427d44e5c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432647} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2337a1-6657-5fe8-b450-b5748ff50406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3517e5b4-f44f-5fdf-86dc-8c2764fac2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432611} :answer-field/id #uuid "827e9169-511c-5ab4-8026-8070fee5eb71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8aae46a7-7405-5484-9202-26c3555ea95d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107832" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z}}{t{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral II matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z}}{t{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral III matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1856db13-851f-5360-910f-bf03676b767e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432605} {:answer/id #uuid "84881f19-e080-51af-af16-c6329de4fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432633} {:answer/id #uuid "b379c0cc-13d0-5691-88bb-9751f4bddd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432646} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b3b242-9319-53aa-a973-839696610da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432657} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84e2ce-01a3-5748-b7d2-641711b82cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc84e2ce-01a3-5748-b7d2-641711b82cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432671} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d3a595-3c97-56e6-b4e9-37484ca30241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2346f7c5-c1ac-509c-ac43-d1a23908e3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-107833" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{z-1}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition.  The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14bc919f-37b1-5bfd-9092-4f4fec1c9b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432602} {:answer/id #uuid "45349eef-8d3d-5379-9b8b-6f793b4003b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432613} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6629d0-7785-58c3-802f-ce326ee02b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432629} {:answer/id #uuid "9db4a047-e2d4-57ce-acfe-2fdba69d4b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432641} {:answer/id #uuid "f33dd163-2dc5-5312-88c8-a108bb20e78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45349eef-8d3d-5379-9b8b-6f793b4003b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432613} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6b14e4-15be-5db4-bf56-15f3f68a2ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef7933ca-459a-5897-8c5b-5b08b877b68b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107838" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{3z-1}}{{e}^{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. $3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition. We use a substitution $s={t}^{3},$ then $3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\text{d}s.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)+2}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}(3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{s}^{z-1}{e}^{-s}\\,\\text{d}s=Γ(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59f9abf1-17db-5717-8660-a531ef00b15c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function for a Positive Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339fc1c0-3378-5749-a840-c336809f200e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432610} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f4d46-fb0d-5d75-9842-205230ceda38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432628} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5e3128-4f90-525f-817e-95775bdcff3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :